which is good to store  password in string or char[] ?
--->
In Java, it is better to store passwords in char[] rather than String.
Why char[] is preferred 🔐
1.Immutability of String

String objects are immutable.

Once created, the password cannot be changed or cleared.

It stays in memory until garbage-collected, and you cannot control when that happens.

This increases the risk of the password being exposed via:
Memory dumps
Heap inspection
Debuggers

String password = "secret123"; // stays in memory

2. char[] can be wiped explicitly
A char[] is mutable, so you can overwrite it once you’re done.
This reduces the time the password exists in memory.
char[] password = {'s','e','c','r','e','t'};
Arrays.fill(password, '\0'); // clear after use


