if we using the set with primitive then duplicates not allowed 
but if we are using set with any object then it allowed duplicates to avoid this there must be equals and hashcode method  override in class
Set<Person> s = new Set<>();  here for same id we can add two entry and it will get print also so to avoid this provide hashcode and euqals method in person class

A Set does not allow duplicates, but it decides what a duplicate is using:
👉 equals() and hashCode()
If you don’t override these methods in your object, Java uses the default implementation from Object.

-----Default behavior (why duplicates appear)

By default:
class Person {
    String name;
}

Set<Person> set = new HashSet<>();

set.add(new Person("Alice"));
set.add(new Person("Alice"));

System.out.println(set.size()); // 2 😮

----Why?

Each new Person() has a different memory address
Default equals() → compares references (==)
Default hashCode() → based on memory address
Person@1a2b3c ≠ Person@4d5e6f


----How Set really checks duplicates

For HashSet:
Compare hashCode()
If same, compare equals()
if (obj1.hashCode() == obj2.hashCode())
    then obj1.equals(obj2)
Only if equals() returns true → duplicate

----Correct way: Override equals() and hashCode()
class Person {
    String name;

    Person(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof Person)) return false;
        Person person = (Person) o;
        return Objects.equals(name, person.name);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(name);
    }
}

so in short --> 
Set prevents duplicates based on equals() and hashCode().
Without overriding them, each object is treated as unique.”


*
fail fast -> A fail-fast iterator immediately throws an exception if the collection is structurally modified during iteration 
(except through the iterator’s own remove() method).
	ex-> ArrayList , HashMap , vector

Key points

Detects concurrent modification
Throws ConcurrentModificationException
Does not guarantee safety in multi-threaded environments
Works on the original collection

ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add(1);
list.add(2);
list.add(3);

Iterator<Integer> it = list.iterator();
while (it.hasNext()) {
    Integer val = it.next();
    list.add(4); // Structural modification
}
//it will throw exception

fail safe -> A fail-safe iterator does not throw an exception if the collection is modified during iteration.
  	ex->CopyOnWriteArrayList, CopyOnWriteArraySet, ConcurrentHashMap
  	
Key points
Works on a copy (snapshot) of the collection
Safe in concurrent environments
Does not reflect modifications made after iteration starts
Slightly more memory overhead

CopyOnWriteArrayList<Integer> list = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();
list.add(1);
list.add(2);
list.add(3);

Iterator<Integer> it = list.iterator();
while (it.hasNext()) {
    Integer val = it.next();
    list.add(4); // No exception
}


internal working --->
Fail-fast iterators use modCount and throw an exception on concurrent structural modification, whereas fail-safe iterators 
iterate over a snapshot or weakly consistent structure, allowing concurrent modification without failure.

fail fast internal-->
Most non-concurrent collections in java.util inherit from AbstractList, AbstractMap, or similar base classes.
They contain an internal field ->
protected transient int modCount = 0;

What modCount tracks-->
it tracks Structural modifications:
add()
remove()
clear()
put() (for maps)
Every structural change increments modCount.

When an iterator is created:
Iterator<E> it = list.iterator();
The iterator stores a snapshot of modCount: like below 
int expectedModCount = modCount;
This value is local to the iterator, not shared.

Every call to:
next()
remove()
hasNext() (sometimes indirectly)
invokes:

final void checkForComodification() {
    if (modCount != expectedModCount)
        throw new ConcurrentModificationException();
}

What this means
If any other thread or same thread modifies the collection
And it does NOT go through the iterator
modCount changes → mismatch → fail immediately
This is why it’s called fail-fast.

Fail-Fast Is NOT Thread-Safe -> because modCount is not volatile


Fail safe internals --->
Fail-safe iterators are implemented in java.util.concurrent package.

Key design principle:
Iterate over immutable snapshot, modify original safely

CopyOnWriteArrayList
Internal structure:

private transient volatile Object[] array;
On write (add, remove)
Acquire lock
Copy the entire array
Modify the new array
Assign it back to array

Object[] newArray = Arrays.copyOf(oldArray, oldArray.length + 1);
newArray[last] = element;
array = newArray;

next() simply reads from snapshot
No modCount, no checks, no exceptions.


Iterator references old array
Modifications create a new array
Snapshot never changes

Why Java Chose Fail-Fast by Default

Performance (no copying)
Memory efficiency
Bug detection during development
Most collections are single-thread optimized
Fail-safe is reserved for high-concurrency use cases.



