hashmap and concurrenthash map

hashmap ->
HashMap is a non-synchronized implementation of the Map interface.
It stores data as key–value pairs and uses hashing to store and retrieve elements efficiently.
not thread safe 
allow one key null and multiple values can be null
Faster in single-threaded environments
Not safe when multiple threads access it at the same time
Internally uses an array of buckets
linkedlist till java 7
Linked List → Red-Black Tree when many collisions occur (Java 8+)


concurrentHashMap->
ConcurrentHashMap is a thread-safe implementation of the Map interface
 designed for high-performance concurrent access.
 dont allow null keys or null values
 Allows multiple threads to read and write simultaneously
 
Java 7 (Segment-based locking)
Map divided into segments
Each segment had its own lock
Multiple threads could access different segments at the same time

Java 8+ (No segments)
Uses:
CAS (Compare-And-Swap)
Fine-grained locking
Locks only specific buckets, not the entire map
Much better scalability


| Feature         | HashMap              | ConcurrentHashMap     |
| --------------- | -------------------- | --------------------- |
| Thread-safe     | ❌ No                 | ✅ Yes                 |
| Synchronization | None                 | Internal fine-grained |
| Performance     | Fast (single thread) | Fast (multi-threaded) |
| Null key        | ✅ One allowed        | ❌ Not allowed         |
| Null values     | ✅ Allowed            | ❌ Not allowed         |
| Iterators       | Fail-fast            | Fail-safe             |
| Locking         | No locking           | Bucket-level locking  |
| Use case        | Single-threaded apps | Multi-threaded apps   |



*
if hashtable which is already synchronized then why we need concurrenthashmap?

-->
coarse- grained locking -> Single lock for the entire map ,Only one thread can read/write at a time,High contention
                           Poor scalability on multi-core CPUs

fine-grained locking -> concurrency technique where locks are applied to small portions of the data instead of the whole map, 
                        allowing multiple threads to operate simultaneously with minimal blocking.  Only one bucket is locked, not entire map

Hashtable is synchronized at method level (coarse-grained locking), which makes it slow.
ConcurrentHashMap uses fine-grained locking and non-blocking techniques, so it is much faster and scalable in multi-threaded environments.

Although Hashtable is synchronized, it uses a single lock for the entire map, which severely limits concurrency. ConcurrentHashMap uses 
fine-grained locking and non-blocking reads, allowing multiple threads to access and modify the map simultaneously with much better performance. 
That’s why ConcurrentHashMap is preferred over Hashtable in modern multi-threaded applications.

in hashtable there is method level locking 
means every method is synchronized 
Only ONE thread can access the map at a time
Even read operations block each other
Causes heavy contention when many threads are involved
If 100 threads want to:
90 → read
10 → write
👉 Still only one thread can execute any method at a time

Hashtable
Iterators are fail-fast
Modifying while iterating → ConcurrentModificationException


in concurrenthashMap
Locks only a small portion of the map   means ->
Imagine a map divided into sections (buckets / segments).
Thread-1 updates section A
Thread-2 updates section B

Multiple threads can:
Read simultaneously
Write to different buckets simultaneously

ConcurrentHashMap
Iterators are fail-safe
Allows concurrent modification
No exception


